   "text": {
    "L1": "An object keeps moving, but that alone does not make its motion uniform.",
    "L2": "Uniform linear motion means equal distances in equal intervals of time along a straight line.",
    "L3": "For example, one metre in each equal interval shows uniform motion.",
    "L4": "Now consider a scooter covering 0.5, 0.5, 0.6 and 0.4 kilometres each minute.",
    "L5": "These distances are not all equal, so the scooter's motion is non-uniform.",
    "L6": "The key is to compare distances in equal time intervals, not just the total distance.",
    "L7": "For non-uniform motion, average speed is total distance divided by total time.",
    "L8": "Now choose the motion that is uniform."
   },
